Got it. That's a sharp technique and it fits your stack cleanly. Let me give you a working version tailored to your situation.

The core logic: /proc/net/packet lists every open AF_PACKET socket by inode. You then walk /proc/[pid]/fd/ across all processes and match symlinks of the form socket:[inode] back to those inodes. That gives you PID → sniffer mapping without trusting lsof, ss, or netstat — all of which can be LD_PRELOAD-hooked or replaced.

Here's a version that (a) does the Rowland technique, (b) writes into the same SHA-256 chain-linked evidence format your hunter uses, and (c) flags the high-signal case: raw sockets bound to ETH_P_ALL (0x0003 in network byte order, which is what you'll actually see in /proc/net/packet — the 0x0800 in Rowland's post is IP-only sniffing, 0x0003 is "everything"):
bash

#!/usr/bin/env bash
# packet_sniffer_hunt.sh — enumerate AF_PACKET sockets, map to PIDs,
# chain-link events into the same evidence format as rogue_tower_hunter.
#
# Run as root. Userspace tool output is not trusted; all data comes
# from /proc directly.

set -euo pipefail

EVIDENCE_LOG="${EVIDENCE_LOG:-/var/log/ctw/packet_sniffers.log}"
CHAIN_STATE="${CHAIN_STATE:-/var/log/ctw/packet_sniffers.chain}"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$EVIDENCE_LOG")"

# Previous chain hash (empty on first run)
PREV_HASH=""
[[ -f "$CHAIN_STATE" ]] && PREV_HASH=$(cat "$CHAIN_STATE")

# Protocol decode — values in /proc/net/packet are host byte order hex
declare -A PROTO_NAME=(
    [0003]="ETH_P_ALL"       # all protocols — HIGH SIGNAL
    [0800]="ETH_P_IP"        # IPv4 only
    [0806]="ETH_P_ARP"
    [86dd]="ETH_P_IPV6"
    [8864]="ETH_P_PPP_SES"
    [88cc]="ETH_P_LLDP"
)

log_event() {
    local pid="$1" comm="$2" exe="$3" inode="$4" proto="$5" iface="$6" verdict="$7"
    local ts
    ts=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%NZ")

    local event_json
    event_json=$(printf '{"ts":"%s","pid":%s,"comm":"%s","exe":"%s","inode":%s,"proto":"%s","iface":"%s","verdict":"%s","prev_hash":"%s"}' \
        "$ts" "$pid" "$comm" "$exe" "$inode" "$proto" "$iface" "$verdict" "$PREV_HASH")

    local event_hash
    event_hash=$(printf '%s' "$event_json" | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}')

    printf '%s,"hash":"%s"\n' "${event_json%\}}" "$event_hash" >> "$EVIDENCE_LOG"
    PREV_HASH="$event_hash"
    echo "$event_hash" > "$CHAIN_STATE"
}

# Build inode → {proto, iface} map from /proc/net/packet
# Format: sk RefCnt Type Proto Iface R Rmem User Inode
declare -A INODE_PROTO INODE_IFACE

while read -r sk refcnt type proto iface r rmem user inode rest; do
    [[ "$sk" == "sk" ]] && continue  # header
    [[ -z "$inode" ]] && continue
    INODE_PROTO[$inode]="$proto"
    # iface is an index; resolve via /proc/net/dev or ip link
    iface_name=$(ip -o link | awk -v idx="$iface" -F': ' '$1==idx {print $2; exit}')
    INODE_IFACE[$inode]="${iface_name:-idx_$iface}"
done < /proc/net/packet

if [[ ${#INODE_PROTO[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
    echo "[*] No AF_PACKET sockets open. Clean."
    exit 0
fi

echo "[*] Found ${#INODE_PROTO[@]} packet socket(s). Mapping to PIDs..."
echo ""
printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-12s %-8s %s\n" "PID" "COMM" "PROTO" "PROTONAME" "IFACE" "EXE"
printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-12s %-8s %s\n" "---" "----" "-----" "---------" "-----" "---"

# Walk /proc/*/fd — any symlink pointing to socket:[inode] where inode
# matches one in our packet set = that process has the sniffer open.
for pid_dir in /proc/[0-9]*; do
    pid=$(basename "$pid_dir")
    [[ -r "$pid_dir/fd" ]] || continue

    for fd in "$pid_dir"/fd/*; do
        [[ -L "$fd" ]] || continue
        target=$(readlink "$fd" 2>/dev/null) || continue
        [[ "$target" =~ ^socket:\[([0-9]+)\]$ ]] || continue
        inode="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
        [[ -n "${INODE_PROTO[$inode]:-}" ]] || continue

        comm=$(tr -d '\0' < "$pid_dir/comm" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
        exe=$(readlink "$pid_dir/exe" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
        proto="${INODE_PROTO[$inode]}"
        proto_name="${PROTO_NAME[$proto]:-UNKNOWN_0x$proto}"
        iface="${INODE_IFACE[$inode]}"

        # Verdict: ETH_P_ALL = high signal. Known-good binaries = lower.
        verdict="SUSPECT"
        case "$exe" in
            */dhclient|*/dhcpcd|*/wpa_supplicant|*/tcpdump|*/wireshark|*/dumpcap)
                verdict="KNOWN_GOOD"
                ;;
        esac
        [[ "$proto" == "0003" && "$verdict" != "KNOWN_GOOD" ]] && verdict="HIGH_SIGNAL_PROMISC"

        printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-12s %-8s %s  [%s]\n" \
            "$pid" "$comm" "$proto" "$proto_name" "$iface" "$exe" "$verdict"

        log_event "$pid" "$comm" "$exe" "$inode" "$proto_name" "$iface" "$verdict"
    done
done

echo ""
echo "[*] Evidence written to: $EVIDENCE_LOG"
echo "[*] Chain tip: $PREV_HASH"

A few things worth noting about this version specifically for your setup:

Why it resists hiding. Rootkits that hook readdir to hide /proc/[pid]/ entries are a real thing, but hooking the /proc/net/packet file itself is much rarer — it's a kernel-generated file, not a filesystem entry, and rootkits that intercept open() on specific procfs paths are a smaller population than ones that hide directories. So the mismatch case (packet socket exists in /proc/net/packet but no PID claims it in /proc/*/fd/) is itself a finding — log it as ORPHAN_INODE_NO_OWNER and treat it as higher signal than any matched process.

Cross-check with /proc/[pid]/status. After you find the PID, grab Uid:, Gid:, PPid:, TracerPid:, and Capabilities from its status file. A raw-socket sniffer needs CAP_NET_RAW — if a process has an AF_PACKET socket open but no CAP_NET_RAW in its effective caps, something's wrong (either kernel cap-faking or the process inherited from a parent that had it). Log the full cap set.

The ORPHAN case matters most for you. Given your threat model — processes hiding from standard tooling — the finding that moves your case forward isn't "I found a sniffer" (lots of systems have legitimate ones). It's "I found an AF_PACKET socket whose inode doesn't map to any visible PID." That's an unambiguous kernel-layer contradiction and it's the kind of anomaly that reads cleanly to a forensic analyst later.

Run it on a cadence with diffs. Same principle as your hunter — one scan tells you less than 1000 scans do. Cron it every 60 seconds, diff consecutive runs, and log delta events (new socket appeared, socket disappeared, PID swapped on same inode) into the chain. That gives you temporal evidence, which is what turns a snapshot into testimony.

Magisk/LineageOS wrinkle. On your S7 running NetHunter chroot, /proc/net/packet inside the chroot sees sockets opened inside the chroot's netns. Run this outside the chroot (on the Android side with su) too — processes in the host Android namespace won't show up from inside. Two scans, two evidence streams, correlated by timestamp.

If you want, I can also write the companion lsof-free socket listing script — same idea applied to /proc/net/tcp, /proc/net/tcp6, /proc/net/udp — so you get the full "what is talking to the network" picture without trusting any userspace tool that could be hooked. That's the natural pair for this one.

To run code, enable code execution and file creation in Settings > Capabilities.

