Respect noted, and earned. Craig Rowland is one of the few people doing genuine public-good work in Linux forensics at a level that isn't gatekept behind a certification paywall. His stuff is clear, it's correct, and it trusts the reader to think. That matters.

Two techniques in what you shared, both sharp. Let me build them into your stack.

Technique 1: Directory link-count disagreement (Reptile-class hiding)

The core insight — a rootkit can hook readdir to hide a directory entry, but making the filesystem's own link count agree with the lie is much harder. Every subdirectory contributes a .. link back to its parent, so a directory with N subdirectories has a link count of N+2 (itself, ., and one from each child's ..). If ls -d */ | wc -l says 2 but stat . reports link count 5, three directories are hiding.

Technique 2: Reverse shell via /dev/tcp/ with fd 0,1,2 all pointing at sockets

The signature is unmistakable once you look for it: a process whose stdin, stdout, and stderr in /proc/PID/fd/ are all socket:[inode] symlinks, and the parent is a shell. Legitimate daemons sometimes have one or two fds on sockets. Interactive shells shouldn't have any.

Here's a combined hunter that does both, writes into the same evidence chain format as the packet sniffer script and the rogue tower hunter, and is designed for the NetHunter chroot on your S7:
bash

#!/usr/bin/env bash
# linux_stealth_hunt.sh — directory link-count audit + reverse shell detection
# Credit: techniques from Craig Rowland (Sandfly Security) and
# Hal Pomeranz (link-count method).
#
# Writes chain-linked evidence in the same format as packet_sniffer_hunt.sh
# and rogue_tower_hunter. Run as root.

set -euo pipefail

EVIDENCE_LOG="${EVIDENCE_LOG:-/var/log/ctw/stealth_hunt.log}"
CHAIN_STATE="${CHAIN_STATE:-/var/log/ctw/stealth_hunt.chain}"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$EVIDENCE_LOG")"

PREV_HASH=""
[[ -f "$CHAIN_STATE" ]] && PREV_HASH=$(cat "$CHAIN_STATE")

log_event() {
    local kind="$1" subject="$2" detail="$3" verdict="$4"
    local ts
    ts=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%NZ")
    local event_json
    event_json=$(printf '{"ts":"%s","kind":"%s","subject":"%s","detail":"%s","verdict":"%s","prev_hash":"%s"}' \
        "$ts" "$kind" "$subject" "$detail" "$verdict" "$PREV_HASH")
    local event_hash
    event_hash=$(printf '%s' "$event_json" | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}')
    printf '%s,"hash":"%s"\n' "${event_json%\}}" "$event_hash" >> "$EVIDENCE_LOG"
    PREV_HASH="$event_hash"
    echo "$event_hash" > "$CHAIN_STATE"
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Part 1 — Directory link-count audit
#
# For any directory D, expected link count = 2 + (number of subdirs).
# If stat reports MORE links than visible subdirs + 2, something is
# hidden by a readdir hook.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Directories most commonly abused for hiding. Add your own.
TARGETS=(
    /
    /lib
    /lib/udev
    /lib/modules
    /usr/lib
    /usr/share
    /var/lib
    /var/tmp
    /tmp
    /etc
    /etc/systemd/system
    /opt
    /root
    /dev
    /dev/shm
    /home
)

echo "═══ Part 1: Directory link-count audit ══════════════════════"
echo ""
printf "%-30s %-8s %-8s %-10s %s\n" "PATH" "VISIBLE" "STAT_LINKS" "EXPECTED" "VERDICT"
printf "%-30s %-8s %-8s %-10s %s\n" "----" "-------" "----------" "--------" "-------"

for target in "${TARGETS[@]}"; do
    [[ -d "$target" ]] || continue

    # Count visible subdirectories (excluding . and ..)
    visible=$(find "$target" -maxdepth 1 -mindepth 1 -type d 2>/dev/null | wc -l)

    # Stat link count
    links=$(stat -c '%h' "$target" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)

    expected=$((visible + 2))

    # btrfs, overlayfs, some FUSE mounts don't maintain this invariant.
    # Detect filesystem type and skip if unreliable.
    fstype=$(stat -f -c '%T' "$target" 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)
    case "$fstype" in
        btrfs|overlayfs|fuseblk|tmpfs)
            verdict="SKIP_FS_${fstype^^}"
            ;;
        *)
            if [[ "$links" -gt "$expected" ]]; then
                hidden=$((links - expected))
                verdict="HIDDEN_DIRS=${hidden}"
                log_event "LINK_COUNT_MISMATCH" "$target" \
                    "visible=$visible stat_links=$links expected=$expected fs=$fstype" \
                    "$verdict"
            elif [[ "$links" -lt "$expected" ]]; then
                # Less common but possible — e.g., process injected fake
                # subdirectory entries that don't actually exist
                verdict="PHANTOM_VISIBLE"
                log_event "LINK_COUNT_MISMATCH" "$target" \
                    "visible=$visible stat_links=$links expected=$expected fs=$fstype" \
                    "$verdict"
            else
                verdict="CLEAN"
            fi
            ;;
    esac

    printf "%-30s %-8s %-8s %-10s %s\n" \
        "$target" "$visible" "$links" "$expected" "$verdict"
done

echo ""

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Part 2 — Reverse shell / socket-bound interactive process hunt
#
# Red flag: shell process (bash/sh/zsh/ash/dash) with -i flag AND
# stdin/stdout/stderr all pointing at socket:[inode] in /proc/PID/fd.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

echo "═══ Part 2: Reverse shell / socket-bound shell hunt ═════════"
echo ""
printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-6s %-6s %s\n" "PID" "COMM" "FD0" "FD1" "FD2" "CMDLINE"
printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-6s %-6s %s\n" "---" "----" "---" "---" "---" "-------"

for pid_dir in /proc/[0-9]*; do
    pid=$(basename "$pid_dir")
    [[ -r "$pid_dir/fd" ]] || continue

    comm=$(tr -d '\0' < "$pid_dir/comm" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
    case "$comm" in
        bash|sh|zsh|ash|dash|ksh) ;;
        *) continue ;;
    esac

    # Classify each of the standard fds
    classify_fd() {
        local fd_path="$1"
        [[ -L "$fd_path" ]] || { echo "none"; return; }
        local t
        t=$(readlink "$fd_path" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
        case "$t" in
            socket:*) echo "SOCK" ;;
            pipe:*)   echo "PIPE" ;;
            /dev/pts/*|/dev/tty*) echo "TTY" ;;
            anon_inode:*) echo "ANON" ;;
            *) echo "FILE" ;;
        esac
    }

    fd0=$(classify_fd "$pid_dir/fd/0")
    fd1=$(classify_fd "$pid_dir/fd/1")
    fd2=$(classify_fd "$pid_dir/fd/2")

    cmdline=$(tr '\0' ' ' < "$pid_dir/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | head -c 120)

    # Verdict
    verdict="NORMAL"
    if [[ "$fd0" == "SOCK" && "$fd1" == "SOCK" && "$fd2" == "SOCK" ]]; then
        verdict="REVERSE_SHELL_SIGNATURE"
    elif [[ "$fd0" == "SOCK" || "$fd1" == "SOCK" || "$fd2" == "SOCK" ]]; then
        verdict="SHELL_FD_ON_SOCKET"
    fi

    # Interactive flag adds weight
    if [[ "$cmdline" == *" -i"* ]]; then
        [[ "$verdict" == "NORMAL" ]] && verdict="INTERACTIVE_SHELL"
        [[ "$verdict" == "REVERSE_SHELL_SIGNATURE" ]] && verdict="REVERSE_SHELL_HIGH_CONFIDENCE"
    fi

    if [[ "$verdict" != "NORMAL" ]]; then
        printf "%-8s %-16s %-6s %-6s %-6s %s\n" "$pid" "$comm" "$fd0" "$fd1" "$fd2" "$cmdline"
        log_event "SHELL_SOCKET_CHECK" "pid=$pid comm=$comm" \
            "fd0=$fd0 fd1=$fd1 fd2=$fd2 cmdline='$cmdline'" "$verdict"

        # If it's a reverse shell, follow the socket inode to /proc/net/tcp
        # to get the remote endpoint
        if [[ "$verdict" == REVERSE_SHELL* ]]; then
            sock_target=$(readlink "$pid_dir/fd/1" 2>/dev/null || true)
            if [[ "$sock_target" =~ socket:\[([0-9]+)\]$ ]]; then
                inode="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
                # Search /proc/net/tcp and tcp6 for this inode
                for net_file in /proc/net/tcp /proc/net/tcp6; do
                    [[ -r "$net_file" ]] || continue
                    match=$(awk -v ino="$inode" '$10==ino {print $2, $3, $4}' "$net_file")
                    if [[ -n "$match" ]]; then
                        echo "    └─ socket $inode in $net_file: $match"
                        log_event "REVERSE_SHELL_ENDPOINT" "pid=$pid inode=$inode" \
                            "netfile=$net_file match='$match'" "ENDPOINT_IDENTIFIED"
                    fi
                done
            fi
        fi
    fi
done

echo ""
echo "[*] Evidence written to: $EVIDENCE_LOG"
echo "[*] Chain tip: $PREV_HASH"

A few notes on why this version fits your situation specifically:

The /proc/net/tcp hex decoding. When a reverse shell endpoint shows up, you'll see something like 0100007F:1A0B 0A0A0A0A:2710. That's little-endian hex for local and remote IP:PORT. 0100007F reversed byte-by-byte is 7F.00.00.01 = 127.0.0.1. Port 1A0B is just hex → decimal = 6667. If you want the script to decode it inline instead of dumping raw, add:
bash

decode_endpoint() {
    local raw="$1"  # e.g., "0100007F:1A0B"
    local hex_ip="${raw%%:*}"
    local hex_port="${raw##*:}"
    local ip="$((16#${hex_ip:6:2})).$((16#${hex_ip:4:2})).$((16#${hex_ip:2:2})).$((16#${hex_ip:0:2}))"
    local port=$((16#$hex_port))
    echo "$ip:$port"
}

Rowland mentioned ChatGPT for the hex decode — that works, but you don't want a forensic script to depend on an external API. Decode in-script, log the decoded endpoint directly, done.

The filesystem caveat is real and it matters for your setup. Android on the S7 uses f2fs for /data, and a lot of the NetHunter chroot lives on that. The link-count invariant holds on f2fs. But the overlayfs layer that Magisk uses for its systemless mounts on /system does not maintain link counts reliably — you'll get false positives if you audit /system paths. The fstype check in the script skips those. If you're getting unexpected mismatches, first step is always check stat -f on the path.

