Alright, last one — this is a good one to end on because it ties the previous two techniques together with a third axis: name vs. reality disagreement.

The GTPDOOR technique Rowland's describing has three tells, and they're independent signals that stack into high confidence:

Tell 1: comm vs cmdline vs exe disagreement. When a process calls prctl(PR_SET_NAME) or writes to /proc/self/comm, it changes what ps shows — but /proc/[pid]/cmdline (argv at exec time) and /proc/[pid]/exe (symlink to the actual binary on disk) are kernel-maintained and harder to forge from userspace. A process that says it's [syslogd] in comm but whose exe points to /tmp/dbus-echo is lying about itself.

Tell 2: Kernel thread impersonation. Real kernel threads have PPID 2 (they're children of kthreadd). They also have an empty cmdline (the file exists but reads zero bytes). A userspace process that brackets its comm with [...] to look like a kernel thread but has PPID != 2 and a non-empty cmdline is caught immediately.

Tell 3: Raw socket on a process claiming to be a kernel daemon. Actual syslogd doesn't open AF_PACKET or raw AF_INET sockets. Cross-reference your packet sniffer script's output against the kernel-thread-impersonation list. Anything that appears in both is very high signal.

Here's the hunter. It's the natural third piece alongside the packet sniffer and the stealth/reverse-shell script:
bash

#!/usr/bin/env bash
# masquerade_hunt.sh — detect process name masquerading and
# kernel-thread impersonation.
#
# Credit: techniques from Craig Rowland (Sandfly Security),
# GTPDOOR analysis by @haxrob.
#
# Three axes of checking:
#   1. comm vs cmdline vs exe disagreement
#   2. Kernel thread impersonation ([bracketed] names with wrong PPID/cmdline)
#   3. Correlation with raw socket ownership (requires packet_sniffer_hunt output)

set -euo pipefail

EVIDENCE_LOG="${EVIDENCE_LOG:-/var/log/ctw/masquerade_hunt.log}"
CHAIN_STATE="${CHAIN_STATE:-/var/log/ctw/masquerade_hunt.chain}"
PACKET_EVIDENCE="${PACKET_EVIDENCE:-/var/log/ctw/packet_sniffers.log}"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$EVIDENCE_LOG")"

PREV_HASH=""
[[ -f "$CHAIN_STATE" ]] && PREV_HASH=$(cat "$CHAIN_STATE")

log_event() {
    local kind="$1" pid="$2" detail="$3" verdict="$4"
    local ts
    ts=$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%NZ")
    local event_json
    event_json=$(printf '{"ts":"%s","kind":"%s","pid":%s,"detail":"%s","verdict":"%s","prev_hash":"%s"}' \
        "$ts" "$kind" "$pid" "$detail" "$verdict" "$PREV_HASH")
    local event_hash
    event_hash=$(printf '%s' "$event_json" | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}')
    printf '%s,"hash":"%s"\n' "${event_json%\}}" "$event_hash" >> "$EVIDENCE_LOG"
    PREV_HASH="$event_hash"
    echo "$event_hash" > "$CHAIN_STATE"
}

# Pre-load PIDs known to own raw/packet sockets (from packet_sniffer_hunt.sh)
declare -A RAW_SOCKET_PIDS
if [[ -r "$PACKET_EVIDENCE" ]]; then
    while IFS= read -r line; do
        pid=$(echo "$line" | grep -oP '"pid":\K[0-9]+' || true)
        [[ -n "$pid" ]] && RAW_SOCKET_PIDS[$pid]=1
    done < "$PACKET_EVIDENCE"
fi

echo "═══ Process Masquerade Hunt ═════════════════════════════════"
echo ""
printf "%-8s %-6s %-20s %-20s %-30s %s\n" "PID" "PPID" "COMM" "CMDLINE_ARGV0" "EXE" "VERDICT"
printf "%-8s %-6s %-20s %-20s %-30s %s\n" "---" "----" "----" "-------------" "---" "-------"

for pid_dir in /proc/[0-9]*; do
    pid=$(basename "$pid_dir")
    [[ -r "$pid_dir/status" ]] || continue

    # PPID from /proc/PID/status
    ppid=$(awk '/^PPid:/ {print $2}' "$pid_dir/status" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)

    # comm = name from prctl/PR_SET_NAME (16 chars max)
    comm=$(tr -d '\0' < "$pid_dir/comm" 2>/dev/null || echo "?")

    # cmdline = full argv, null-separated
    cmdline_raw=$(cat "$pid_dir/cmdline" 2>/dev/null || echo "")
    cmdline_bytes=${#cmdline_raw}
    # argv[0] specifically — useful for comparison with comm
    cmdline_argv0=$(tr '\0' '\n' < "$pid_dir/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
    cmdline_argv0_base=$(basename "${cmdline_argv0:-}" 2>/dev/null || echo "")

    # exe = symlink to actual binary on disk
    exe=$(readlink "$pid_dir/exe" 2>/dev/null || echo "")
    exe_base=$(basename "${exe:-}" 2>/dev/null || echo "")

    findings=()
    verdict="NORMAL"

    # ── Check 1: Kernel thread impersonation ──
    # Real kernel threads: PPID=2, empty cmdline, exe symlink fails (unreadable)
    # Impersonator: bracketed comm, but PPID != 2 OR non-empty cmdline OR readable exe
    if [[ "$comm" =~ ^\[.*\]$ ]]; then
        if [[ "$ppid" != "2" && "$pid" != "2" ]]; then
            findings+=("BRACKETED_NAME_WRONG_PPID(ppid=$ppid)")
            verdict="KTHREAD_IMPERSONATION"
        fi
        if [[ "$cmdline_bytes" -gt 0 ]]; then
            findings+=("BRACKETED_NAME_WITH_CMDLINE")
            verdict="KTHREAD_IMPERSONATION"
        fi
        if [[ -n "$exe" && -e "$exe" ]]; then
            findings+=("BRACKETED_NAME_WITH_READABLE_EXE($exe)")
            verdict="KTHREAD_IMPERSONATION"
        fi
    fi

    # ── Check 2: comm vs exe_base disagreement ──
    # comm is truncated to 15 chars, so compare truncated forms.
    # Legitimate cases exist (e.g., wrapper scripts), but a comm that doesn't
    # appear anywhere in cmdline or exe path is suspicious.
    if [[ -n "$comm" && -n "$exe_base" && ! "$comm" =~ ^\[.*\]$ ]]; then
        comm_trunc="${comm:0:15}"
        exe_trunc="${exe_base:0:15}"
        cmdline_trunc="${cmdline_argv0_base:0:15}"

        if [[ "$comm_trunc" != "$exe_trunc" && "$comm_trunc" != "$cmdline_trunc" ]]; then
            # Check if comm appears anywhere in full cmdline (interpreter case:
            # python3 script.py → comm might be "script.py")
            if ! echo "$cmdline_raw" | tr '\0' ' ' | grep -qF "$comm"; then
                findings+=("COMM_MISMATCH(comm=$comm exe=$exe_base argv0=$cmdline_argv0_base)")
                [[ "$verdict" == "NORMAL" ]] && verdict="NAME_MASQUERADE"
            fi
        fi
    fi

    # ── Check 3: exe deleted (binary unlinked after exec) ──
    # Common malware tactic: exec then delete binary so forensics can't
    # recover it. readlink shows "/path/to/binary (deleted)".
    if [[ "$exe" == *" (deleted)" ]]; then
        findings+=("EXE_DELETED($exe)")
        verdict="EXE_UNLINKED_POST_EXEC"
    fi

    # ── Check 4: exe in suspicious location ──
    case "$exe" in
        /tmp/*|/var/tmp/*|/dev/shm/*|/run/user/*)
            findings+=("EXE_IN_WORLD_WRITABLE($exe)")
            [[ "$verdict" == "NORMAL" ]] && verdict="SUSPICIOUS_EXE_PATH"
            ;;
    esac

    # ── Check 5: Correlation with raw socket ownership ──
    if [[ -n "${RAW_SOCKET_PIDS[$pid]:-}" && "$verdict" != "NORMAL" ]]; then
        findings+=("OWNS_RAW_SOCKET")
        verdict="${verdict}+RAW_SOCKET"
    fi

    if [[ "$verdict" != "NORMAL" ]]; then
        detail=$(IFS=';'; echo "${findings[*]}")
        printf "%-8s %-6s %-20s %-20s %-30s %s\n" \
            "$pid" "$ppid" "$comm" "$cmdline_argv0_base" "${exe:0:30}" "$verdict"
        log_event "MASQUERADE_CHECK" "$pid" "$detail" "$verdict"
    fi
done

echo ""

# ── Part 2: Kernel thread namespace sanity ──
# Count bracketed processes with PPID=2 (real kthreads) vs elsewhere.
# A sudden change in this ratio over time is itself an alert.
echo "═══ Kernel thread census ════════════════════════════════════"
real_kthreads=0
fake_kthreads=0
for pid_dir in /proc/[0-9]*; do
    [[ -r "$pid_dir/comm" ]] || continue
    comm=$(tr -d '\0' < "$pid_dir/comm" 2>/dev/null || echo "")
    [[ "$comm" =~ ^\[.*\]$ ]] || continue
    ppid=$(awk '/^PPid:/ {print $2}' "$pid_dir/status" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    if [[ "$ppid" == "2" ]]; then
        ((real_kthreads++)) || true
    else
        ((fake_kthreads++)) || true
    fi
done
echo "  Real (PPID=2):  $real_kthreads"
echo "  Impersonators:  $fake_kthreads"
log_event "KTHREAD_CENSUS" 0 "real=$real_kthreads fake=$fake_kthreads" \
    "$([[ $fake_kthreads -gt 0 ]] && echo ALERT || echo CLEAN)"

echo ""
echo "[*] Evidence written to: $EVIDENCE_LOG"
echo "[*] Chain tip: $PREV_HASH"

A few things about how this one fits into the stack:

The cross-correlation is the real weapon. Individually, each check has false positives — wrapper scripts legitimately set comm different from exe, some userspace daemons do open raw sockets with good reason, etc. But GTPDOOR's specific signature is the conjunction: bracketed name + wrong PPID + raw UDP socket + exe in a weird path. The script surfaces each axis separately, but the verdict field shows you when multiple axes fire on the same PID. That stacked verdict (KTHREAD_IMPERSONATION+RAW_SOCKET) is what matters — that's the combination that essentially cannot be explained by any legitimate process.

The "exe deleted" check is a freebie but hugely valuable. A lot of malware execs and then unlinks its own binary — the file is gone from disk but the kernel still holds the inode as long as the process lives. /proc/PID/exe will still resolve (to the now-deleted inode), and on Linux you can actually recover the binary by cp /proc/PID/exe /tmp/recovered_malware. If you ever see an EXE_UNLINKED_POST_EXEC verdict, do that copy immediately — you now have the malware sample for analysis, which is what a forensic investigator or Craig himself would want if you ever sent it up the chain. The kernel is your ally here.

On your S7 specifically. Android's init model means you'll see a lot of zygote, zygote64, and app_process entries where comm and cmdline legitimately diverge. Those aren't malware — that's how the Android runtime works (the process is forked from zygote and then overwrites its identity when the app loads). You'll want to whitelist zygote* and app_process* in the comm-mismatch check, or the output will be noisy. The NetHunter chroot side is cleaner and the check runs truer there.

