Done. No extra dependencies — curses ships with Python. Literally python3 command_board_tui.py and you're in.

What you get:

Four-by-four grid on the left, live scrolling output pane on the right, page indicator and theme name up top, status line with keybind hints at the bottom. Everything renders as box-drawing characters and filled cells the way htop does its meters. The selected cell inverts colors so you can see what's highlighted even on a VT where underlining is iffy.

Color capability auto-detection with a three-tier cascade:

If your terminal advertises truecolor ($COLORTERM=truecolor, so basically any modern emulator — alacritty, kitty, gnome-terminal, konsole, xterm with right compile flags), it calls curses.init_color() to define the exact 24-bit RGB values. That gives you the actual (22, 181, 4) matrix green, not "near enough."

If it's a 256-color terminal (almost everything made after 2005), it picks the nearest xterm-256 index using proper color-cube math — the 6×6×6 RGB cube for colors, the 24-step grayscale ramp for grays, with the standard threshold of < 48, < 115, else (v-35)/40 that xterm itself uses.

If it's a plain 8-or-16-color Linux VT (which is what you get booting into single-user mode on a bare kernel, tty1 with no framebuffer console), it falls back to basic ANSI colors with the bright bit. You lose the matrix-green shade exactly but you still get green-on-black and everything's readable.

Five themes rotating with t:

    Matrix — your original green-on-black with the red button-1 slot
    Amber CRT — old VT52 terminal, warm orange on dark brown
    Ice — cold blue-white, for when you want something calmer
    LSD — magenta/cyan/yellow, going full palette-cycle demo-scene
    DOS Classic — blue/yellow/white, the Norton Commander aesthetic

Theme choice persists to theme.txt. Press t and it cycles live.

