When you hit a command prefixed with !, the TUI suspends curses, hands the terminal over to the command, lets it run full-TTY (so htop, vim, nano, less, interactive installers, sudo password prompts, all work correctly), then when it exits shows "press Enter to return" and resumes the UI. That's the part that makes it viable as a single-user-mode emergency tool — if you need to !fsck /dev/sda1 or !journalctl -xe, you get the full interactive experience and then come back to your board.

