    Bounds checking is the default: every [i] is either .get(i) returning Option, or pattern matching — no out-of-range indexing possible.
    panic = "abort" in release: crash means the kernel reaps the process cleanly and the TTY driver restores cooked mode on close. No half-cleaned-up terminal.
    Atomic config writes: the atomic_write helper writes-then-renames so kill -9 mid-save can't corrupt your commands.txt.
    Single binary, ~2MB stripped: no Python interpreter at the build-chain boundary — which matters to your threat model specifically.
    Per-line cap in the output pump: a command that writes a gigabyte with no newline can't eat unbounded memory.
    UTF-8-safe truncation everywhere: String::truncate panics on bad char boundaries; I use is_char_boundary walkback.
