The new layout you asked for:

Instead of modal popups for editing, the edit panel is now docked below the button grid — always visible, always in sync with the currently-selected slot. Looks like:

  ■ slot 05  cmd: [ls -la           ]  desc: [list files     ]

Tab cycles focus: grid → command field → description field → grid. Shift-Tab reverses. Typing in a field updates that slot live with no "save this edit?" step — what you see in the field is what's in the slot. When a field has focus it gets a yellow-bordered [ ] and a cursor block; when it doesn't, it's just dim brackets showing what the slot currently contains. The output pane on the right, the 4×4 grid, and the fields are all visible at once.

The page description is still modal via p because you rarely touch it. Theme cycling is still t. All the other controls from before still work.

Bugs fixed from my last pass (the honest list):

    String::truncate-style UTF-8 panics — in the Rust version walks back to nearest char boundary; in Python they don't happen naturally but I still cap length consistently.
    Dead select and signal imports removed.
    Dead running_processes list removed (it was a race hazard that served no purpose).
    output_scroll now correctly reduces by drop when the log trims, so your scrolled-back view stays pinned to the same content instead of silently drifting.
    Output-wrapping is now tail-driven — only wraps as many lines as are actually visible + scroll offset. The Python version was re-wrapping the entire 5000-line log every frame at 20fps. Now it stops as soon as it has enough.
    curses.raw() failure now falls back to cbreak() instead of silently doing neither.
    Config directory uses the $COMMAND_BOARD_DIR → pwd → $HOME/.command_board → /tmp cascade instead of __file__.resolve().parent which lies about symlinks.
    Near-white (r,g,b > 240) now snaps to xterm color 231 (pure white) instead of muddy gray 23.
    All config writes are atomic — temp file + os.replace(). Kill -9 mid-save can't corrupt commands.txt anymore.
    Output pump has per-line cap so a command that writes a gigabyte with no newline can't eat unbounded memory.
    Control chars stripped from command output — no ANSI-injection from child processes.
    handle_key paths split cleanly (handle_key_grid vs handle_key_field) so the state machine is actually a state machine, not a nested if-soup.

